1
00:00:00,660 --> 00:00:02,840
Hello everyone and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:03,060 --> 00:00:03,480
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:03,980 --> 00:00:08,380
My name is Catherine and you've probably heard us talking a little bit about small talk the past few weeks.

4
00:00:08,420 --> 00:00:16,140
We have another episode about small talk and we're going to teach you some things that you can say to people when you haven't seen them or maybe even met them before.

5
00:00:16,460 --> 00:00:16,760
That's right.

6
00:00:16,960 --> 00:00:18,280
Today we're going to talk to a stranger.

7
00:00:18,900 --> 00:00:22,580
So we're going to talk very briefly about some casual topic.

8
00:00:23,800 --> 00:00:28,000
And so let's jump into the dialogue and see what happens.

9
00:00:33,930 --> 00:00:35,770
Excuse me, is this seat taken?

10
00:00:36,210 --> 00:00:37,350
No, please feel free.

11
00:00:37,790 --> 00:00:38,590
Thanks a lot.

12
00:00:39,050 --> 00:00:39,990
Do you work in Shanghai?

13
00:00:40,630 --> 00:00:41,430
Yes, I do.

14
00:00:41,970 --> 00:00:42,550
How about you?

15
00:00:42,930 --> 00:00:43,590
No, I'm a tourist.

16
00:00:44,230 --> 00:00:45,130
This place is amazing.

17
00:00:45,730 --> 00:00:47,890
It's much bigger than I imagined and much more exciting.

18
00:00:48,510 --> 00:00:49,670
There's so much to see here.

19
00:00:49,910 --> 00:00:51,070
You can say that again.

20
00:00:51,750 --> 00:00:53,810
It's much more modern than people imagine.

21
00:00:54,470 --> 00:00:55,370
Where are you from?

22
00:00:55,920 --> 00:00:57,320
Well, let's see.

23
00:00:57,350 --> 00:01:01,090
I'm from Kansas originally, a much quieter and more peaceful place than here.

24
00:01:01,310 --> 00:01:01,910
That's for sure.

25
00:01:03,200 --> 00:01:04,840
But I'm living in Paris right now.

26
00:01:04,840 --> 00:01:05,330
Oh, Paris.

27
00:01:05,840 --> 00:01:06,840
Wonderful.

28
00:01:06,950 --> 00:01:08,110
I'd love to visit sometime.

29
00:01:11,380 --> 00:01:12,080
All right, we're back.

30
00:01:12,300 --> 00:01:15,720
So now we're going to take a look at a lot of phrases on this occasion.

31
00:01:15,720 --> 00:01:21,080
So let's go for fluency builder part one.

32
00:01:21,080 --> 00:01:21,360
Fluency builder.

33
00:01:22,080 --> 00:01:23,080
All right.

34
00:01:23,850 --> 00:01:28,730
So the first phrase we have is a very common phrase if you're in a train or on a bus.

35
00:01:29,270 --> 00:01:31,550
You hear the phrase, is this seat taken?

36
00:01:32,050 --> 00:01:32,250
Right.

37
00:01:32,390 --> 00:01:36,990
So Michelle came up to the stranger and said, excuse me, is this seat taken?

38
00:01:37,350 --> 00:01:39,410
That means, is anybody sitting here?

39
00:01:39,750 --> 00:01:41,150
It basically means, can I sit here?

40
00:01:41,470 --> 00:01:41,670
Right.

41
00:01:41,870 --> 00:01:45,130
This is a very polite way of asking if you can sit down in the seat.

42
00:01:45,280 --> 00:01:49,320
You can also say it in a restaurant, at a movie theater, in a classroom.

43
00:01:49,320 --> 00:01:50,350
So remember this one.

44
00:01:50,410 --> 00:01:52,030
This was a really, really common phrase.

45
00:01:52,390 --> 00:01:54,810
So it's a very polite way of saying, can I sit down?

46
00:01:55,050 --> 00:01:55,330
That's right.

47
00:01:55,570 --> 00:01:56,890
You're asking for permission basically.

48
00:01:57,230 --> 00:01:57,950
Is the seat taken?

49
00:01:58,110 --> 00:02:00,590
Because what if a person says, well, no, it's not.

50
00:02:00,910 --> 00:02:02,930
Do you have to say, may I sit down?

51
00:02:03,010 --> 00:02:03,570
May I take a seat?

52
00:02:03,790 --> 00:02:06,290
Well, if they say, I'm sorry, someone is sitting here.

53
00:02:06,450 --> 00:02:09,610
That means that maybe they're waiting for a friend and then no, you can't sit there.

54
00:02:10,230 --> 00:02:16,030
But if they say, sure, like in the dialogue, feel free, then you're really basically able to sit down.

55
00:02:16,030 --> 00:02:16,510
No problem.

56
00:02:16,930 --> 00:02:17,290
Right.

57
00:02:17,570 --> 00:02:20,390
So the stranger answers by saying, no, please feel free.

58
00:02:20,390 --> 00:02:22,110
So he says, no, the seat is not taken.

59
00:02:22,870 --> 00:02:23,910
Please feel free.

60
00:02:24,590 --> 00:02:25,270
What does he mean by this?

61
00:02:25,570 --> 00:02:25,990
Okay.

62
00:02:26,090 --> 00:02:29,790
Feel free is a very unusual phrase in English, but one that you're going to hear a lot.

63
00:02:30,050 --> 00:02:32,650
It basically means do what you want to do.

64
00:02:32,870 --> 00:02:33,190
Okay.

65
00:02:34,160 --> 00:02:35,160
Okay.

66
00:02:35,160 --> 00:02:37,080
So if I want to sit down, I say, is this seat taken?

67
00:02:37,080 --> 00:02:39,990
If you say, feel free, Marco, that means feel free to sit down.

68
00:02:40,330 --> 00:02:40,490
Okay.

69
00:02:40,510 --> 00:02:46,210
So we can complete the sentence by saying, no, please feel free to sit down.

70
00:02:46,370 --> 00:02:48,730
But it's kind of stating the obvious.

71
00:02:49,070 --> 00:02:51,210
You're saying basically feel free to do what you want.

72
00:02:51,410 --> 00:02:51,650
That's right.

73
00:02:51,690 --> 00:02:52,810
But you could use it in other ways.

74
00:02:53,560 --> 00:02:56,760
For example, Hey Marco, are you using this pen right here?

75
00:02:56,760 --> 00:02:57,690
No, no, feel free.

76
00:02:58,030 --> 00:03:00,050
That means feel free to use my pen.

77
00:03:00,470 --> 00:03:00,710
Okay.

78
00:03:00,890 --> 00:03:01,390
Very good.

79
00:03:02,270 --> 00:03:05,810
And so then they started talking about what they do, where they work.

80
00:03:06,630 --> 00:03:08,810
And Michelle said, yes, I work in Shanghai.

81
00:03:09,450 --> 00:03:10,710
How about you?

82
00:03:11,040 --> 00:03:12,080
Oh, how about you?

83
00:03:12,160 --> 00:03:15,000
Now this is a really important construction.

84
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:16,400
This is a really important phrase in English.

85
00:03:16,680 --> 00:03:19,320
And I think a lot of people make mistakes with this.

86
00:03:19,320 --> 00:03:23,360
So how about you means, do you work in Shanghai?

87
00:03:23,880 --> 00:03:25,960
I just said I work in Shanghai, but how about you?

88
00:03:25,960 --> 00:03:26,220
Do you?

89
00:03:26,560 --> 00:03:26,900
Right.

90
00:03:27,160 --> 00:03:27,540
Exactly.

91
00:03:28,960 --> 00:03:30,880
So that's saying, and you, do you work here?

92
00:03:30,880 --> 00:03:32,300
Or and you, what do you do?

93
00:03:32,800 --> 00:03:33,300
How about you?

94
00:03:33,800 --> 00:03:45,400
Now, the common mistake with how about you or using the construction how about is that for some reason, some people seem to think that it means how is it?

95
00:03:46,240 --> 00:03:49,700
So for example, if someone says, how about your weekend?

96
00:03:51,090 --> 00:03:51,650
That's wrong.

97
00:03:52,010 --> 00:03:52,910
No, we don't say that.

98
00:03:53,070 --> 00:03:54,230
How was your weekend?

99
00:03:54,640 --> 00:03:59,440
But in this case, how about you always follows a statement.

100
00:03:59,630 --> 00:03:59,790
Okay.

101
00:03:59,830 --> 00:04:01,370
So yes, I do live in Shanghai.

102
00:04:01,450 --> 00:04:02,470
How about you?

103
00:04:02,900 --> 00:04:04,360
That means, do you live in Shanghai?

104
00:04:04,360 --> 00:04:05,360
Right.

105
00:04:05,360 --> 00:04:08,460
If I say, I would like to eat a hamburger and French fries.

106
00:04:08,460 --> 00:04:08,970
How about you?

107
00:04:09,460 --> 00:04:09,650
How about you?

108
00:04:10,460 --> 00:04:10,770
What would you like to eat?

109
00:04:11,460 --> 00:04:12,460
Exactly.

110
00:04:12,460 --> 00:04:13,990
So remember, that's the way that you use how about.

111
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:20,570
Never say how about your weekend or how about your vacation when you want to ask, how was it?

112
00:04:21,560 --> 00:04:22,560
Don't say that.

113
00:04:22,560 --> 00:04:27,120
No, basically, this is a way to reply to someone when they're telling you about their experience.

114
00:04:27,120 --> 00:04:28,050
You want to talk about yours.

115
00:04:28,880 --> 00:04:29,050
That's right.

116
00:04:29,880 --> 00:04:31,170
Okay, so let's take a break.

117
00:04:31,640 --> 00:04:42,400
Let's review everything and we'll be back with Fluency Builder part two.

118
00:04:42,400 --> 00:04:44,070
Excuse me, is this seat taken?

119
00:04:44,520 --> 00:04:45,630
No, please feel free.

120
00:04:46,030 --> 00:04:46,850
Thanks a lot.

121
00:04:47,330 --> 00:04:48,250
Do you work in Shanghai?

122
00:04:48,890 --> 00:04:49,710
Yes, I do.

123
00:04:50,270 --> 00:04:50,810
How about you?

124
00:04:51,440 --> 00:04:51,870
No, I'm a tourist.

125
00:04:52,720 --> 00:04:53,410
This place is amazing.

126
00:04:54,060 --> 00:04:56,130
It's much bigger than I imagined and much more exciting.

127
00:04:56,770 --> 00:04:57,930
There's so much to see here.

128
00:04:58,190 --> 00:04:59,330
You can say that again.

129
00:05:00,010 --> 00:05:02,070
It's much more modern than people imagine.

130
00:05:02,740 --> 00:05:03,640
Where are you from?

131
00:05:04,240 --> 00:05:05,280
Well, let's see.

132
00:05:05,600 --> 00:05:09,340
I'm from Kansas originally, a much quieter and more peaceful place than here.

133
00:05:09,560 --> 00:05:10,160
That's for sure.

134
00:05:10,760 --> 00:05:11,760
Uh-huh.

135
00:05:11,760 --> 00:05:13,240
But I'm living in Paris right now.

136
00:05:13,240 --> 00:05:13,580
Oh, Paris.

137
00:05:14,240 --> 00:05:15,240
That's wonderful.

138
00:05:15,240 --> 00:05:16,320
I'd love to visit sometime.

139
00:05:19,500 --> 00:05:20,120
All right, we're back.

140
00:05:20,360 --> 00:05:23,960
So now let's continue with some more phrases on Fluency Builder.

141
00:05:23,960 --> 00:05:26,720
Okay, this first phrase is one that I really like to use.

142
00:05:26,720 --> 00:05:28,720
The phrase is from Michelle.

143
00:05:29,040 --> 00:05:31,620
She says, you can say that again.

144
00:05:32,400 --> 00:05:36,560
But she's talking about how Shanghai is so big and so exciting.

145
00:05:36,560 --> 00:05:38,200
And Michelle says, you can say that again.

146
00:05:38,200 --> 00:05:39,680
Now, what does this mean, Marco?

147
00:05:39,680 --> 00:05:40,680
That's right.

148
00:05:40,680 --> 00:05:43,040
So she is basically agreeing with the person.

149
00:05:43,040 --> 00:05:45,040
The stranger says, Shanghai is amazing.

150
00:05:45,040 --> 00:05:46,040
It's great.

151
00:05:46,040 --> 00:05:47,200
There's so much to see.

152
00:05:47,200 --> 00:05:49,240
Michelle says, you can say that again.

153
00:05:49,240 --> 00:05:51,080
It's saying, I agree with you.

154
00:05:51,080 --> 00:05:51,840
You are so right.

155
00:05:52,080 --> 00:05:53,080
I really agree.

156
00:05:53,500 --> 00:05:55,620
All right, so this is very emphatic.

157
00:05:55,920 --> 00:05:59,420
That means that it's really, it's more than just I agree.

158
00:06:00,200 --> 00:06:01,520
I really, really agree.

159
00:06:01,520 --> 00:06:04,760
The person really feels something about this, feels passionately about it.

160
00:06:05,040 --> 00:06:05,380
That's right.

161
00:06:05,620 --> 00:06:09,280
So if you go to the movies and you say, oh, that movie was terrible, you can say.

162
00:06:09,720 --> 00:06:10,820
You can say that again.

163
00:06:11,180 --> 00:06:11,500
Exactly.

164
00:06:12,200 --> 00:06:13,200
It means, oh, I agree.

165
00:06:13,200 --> 00:06:14,100
It was really bad.

166
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:15,200
That's right.

167
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:21,700
Now, when Michelle asked the stranger where he was from, he says, I'm from Kansas originally.

168
00:06:22,680 --> 00:06:23,940
Okay, what's originally?

169
00:06:24,880 --> 00:06:25,300
I'm original?

170
00:06:25,840 --> 00:06:26,840
No.

171
00:06:26,840 --> 00:06:32,810
I'm from Kansas originally means maybe I don't live in Kansas now, but that's where I come from.

172
00:06:33,280 --> 00:06:34,770
Origin, originally.

173
00:06:35,560 --> 00:06:37,360
That means, you know, at the beginning.

174
00:06:37,360 --> 00:06:38,360
Right.

175
00:06:38,360 --> 00:06:43,720
And remember, Kansas, by the way, is one of the states in the United States.

176
00:06:43,720 --> 00:06:47,760
And so he's saying he's from Kansas, but now he lives in Paris.

177
00:06:47,760 --> 00:06:53,160
So that's why you say, I'm originally from Kansas, but I live in Paris.

178
00:06:53,560 --> 00:06:57,500
All right, so Marco, you live here in Shanghai, but where do you come from originally?

179
00:06:57,880 --> 00:06:59,400
Originally, I'm from Ecuador.

180
00:06:59,820 --> 00:07:00,300
All right.

181
00:07:00,660 --> 00:07:03,660
And I'm originally from Chicago, but I currently live in China.

182
00:07:04,040 --> 00:07:04,520
That's right.

183
00:07:04,940 --> 00:07:05,220
Okay.

184
00:07:06,320 --> 00:07:14,680
And as he was talking about Kansas, he also said that, well, it's obviously a lot quieter and more peaceful.

185
00:07:14,680 --> 00:07:18,260
And then he ends his statement by saying, that's for sure.

186
00:07:19,360 --> 00:07:19,880
That's for sure.

187
00:07:20,360 --> 00:07:23,360
And this is a phrase that we use to add emphasis on something.

188
00:07:24,540 --> 00:07:29,680
It's almost like saying, this is a really quiet, much quieter and peaceful place.

189
00:07:30,240 --> 00:07:30,660
That's for sure.

190
00:07:31,300 --> 00:07:32,020
It really is.

191
00:07:32,460 --> 00:07:38,540
It's not like you're adding anything to the statement, like no meaning, but you're actually kind of making it more strong.

192
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:39,120
Right.

193
00:07:39,800 --> 00:07:40,300
You're saying it's a fact.

194
00:07:40,880 --> 00:07:41,200
It's a fact.

195
00:07:41,880 --> 00:07:42,880
It's really this way.

196
00:07:43,320 --> 00:07:47,130
So obviously, Kansas compared to Shanghai is a lot more quieter.

197
00:07:47,560 --> 00:07:47,910
That's for sure.

198
00:07:48,560 --> 00:07:49,560
Mm-hmm.

199
00:07:49,560 --> 00:07:54,760
Or I could say that in Chicago in the winter, it's a lot colder than Shanghai.

200
00:07:54,760 --> 00:07:55,150
That's for sure.

201
00:07:55,760 --> 00:07:56,760
Right.

202
00:07:56,760 --> 00:08:01,650
So again, you're emphasizing on your statement, telling everyone that it's very, very much like this.

203
00:08:02,160 --> 00:08:03,440
That's how it really is.

204
00:08:03,440 --> 00:08:03,610
Okay.

205
00:08:04,440 --> 00:08:06,430
So that's all the phrases we have for you.

206
00:08:06,680 --> 00:08:16,600
Why don't we listen to our dialogue one last time.

207
00:08:16,600 --> 00:08:17,020
Excuse me.

208
00:08:17,600 --> 00:08:18,320
Is this seat taken?

209
00:08:18,720 --> 00:08:19,880
No, please feel free.

210
00:08:20,280 --> 00:08:21,100
Thanks a lot.

211
00:08:21,560 --> 00:08:22,500
Do you work in Shanghai?

212
00:08:23,120 --> 00:08:23,960
Yes, I do.

213
00:08:24,500 --> 00:08:25,060
How about you?

214
00:08:25,460 --> 00:08:26,120
No, I'm a tourist.

215
00:08:26,820 --> 00:08:27,660
This place is amazing.

216
00:08:28,240 --> 00:08:30,400
It's much bigger than I imagined and much more exciting.

217
00:08:31,020 --> 00:08:32,180
There's so much to see here.

218
00:08:32,420 --> 00:08:33,600
You can say that again.

219
00:08:34,260 --> 00:08:36,320
It's much more modern than people imagine.

220
00:08:36,980 --> 00:08:37,880
Where are you from?

221
00:08:38,480 --> 00:08:39,840
Well, let's see.

222
00:08:39,880 --> 00:08:43,600
I'm from Kansas originally, a much quieter and more peaceful place than here.

223
00:08:43,820 --> 00:08:44,420
That's for sure.

224
00:08:45,040 --> 00:08:46,040
Uh-huh.

225
00:08:46,040 --> 00:08:47,400
But I'm living in Paris right now.

226
00:08:47,400 --> 00:08:47,820
Oh, Paris.

227
00:08:48,400 --> 00:08:49,400
Wonderful.

228
00:08:49,460 --> 00:08:50,620
I'd love to visit sometime.

229
00:08:53,870 --> 00:08:54,350
All right.

230
00:08:54,520 --> 00:08:55,650
So small talk with a stranger.

231
00:08:56,080 --> 00:08:57,970
This is actually a very common scenario.

232
00:08:58,570 --> 00:09:03,810
If in a bus, in a train, you sit next to a stranger, this is more or less what you're going to talk about.

233
00:09:03,990 --> 00:09:04,510
Where are you from?

234
00:09:04,890 --> 00:09:05,430
What do you do?

235
00:09:05,750 --> 00:09:06,190
Where do you live?

236
00:09:06,770 --> 00:09:07,110
That's right.

237
00:09:07,270 --> 00:09:09,590
And you don't even have to talk about this with a total stranger.

238
00:09:09,730 --> 00:09:14,630
You could actually use this with someone that you're working with or just an acquaintance.

239
00:09:14,950 --> 00:09:19,450
So basically, these are key questions that you would ask someone that you don't really know very well.

240
00:09:19,610 --> 00:09:20,050
That's right.

241
00:09:20,190 --> 00:09:24,530
And as you can see, you're not really going into any deep personal questions.

242
00:09:24,790 --> 00:09:29,750
It's very light conversation, very brief as well.

243
00:09:30,170 --> 00:09:30,470
That's right.

244
00:09:31,400 --> 00:09:40,120
And I would like to add that in American culture, people often think it's very rude to ask someone how much do you earn, how much is your apartment, how much is your car.

245
00:09:40,120 --> 00:09:47,680
So in small talk in American culture, and I think a lot of Western cultures, we don't talk about money or religion.

246
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:49,080
So what religion are you?

247
00:09:49,200 --> 00:09:54,000
So I think it's important to talk about things that are very simple.

248
00:09:54,780 --> 00:09:56,280
What do you do for a living?

249
00:09:56,520 --> 00:09:57,060
Where do you live?

250
00:09:58,080 --> 00:09:59,080
How's the weather?

251
00:09:59,080 --> 00:10:00,080
These things.

252
00:10:00,080 --> 00:10:01,080
Yes.

253
00:10:01,080 --> 00:10:07,580
This is exactly, I think, one of the main points that when you're learning a language such as English, not only to understand the language, but also the culture.

254
00:10:07,580 --> 00:10:16,420
It's very, very different, especially, for example, in Asian cultures where it's no big deal if you go to the hairdresser and the hairdresser asks you how much you earn.

255
00:10:16,960 --> 00:10:23,040
But in the United States or many Western countries, it's not something that you talk about your salary or that you really ask.

256
00:10:23,280 --> 00:10:25,040
It's kind of personal information.

257
00:10:25,620 --> 00:10:25,820
It is.

258
00:10:26,020 --> 00:10:29,020
And if you talk about it, you might make people very uncomfortable.

259
00:10:29,600 --> 00:10:36,560
And so I recommend talking about some of the things that we've discussed here and some of the things that we've discussed in other lessons in this series about small talk.

260
00:10:36,740 --> 00:10:37,020
